course: dictionary
lesson: 3187
title: Using encyclopaedias wisely
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An encyclopaedia is a book, or a website, that gives information on many subjects, usually in alphabetical order, so you can look things up quickly. Its entries are the short articles inside it.
explain: Context helps with the word too. «Books and encyclopaedias can be wrong or out of date, so check when they were written.» The sentence tells you that an encyclopaedia is not always right, so you must check it.
explain: Finn trusts his tablet because it is online, and Isla trusts Grandad's old book because it is printed. Both are trusting the container, not checking the claim. A good researcher checks the fact itself, in more than one source.
fact: An encyclopaedia gives information on many subjects, or on many parts of one subject.
fact: Encyclopaedias are usually arranged in alphabetical order.
fact: The US spelling is encyclopedia.
fact: Online encyclopaedias can be edited by anyone, so check facts in more than one source.
fact: «Walking encyclopaedia» is an informal phrase for a person who knows a huge amount.
exercise: What is an encyclopaedia? | A type of loud music | A kind of pet fish | *A book or website giving facts on many subjects | A tool for cooking soup
exercise: «Books and encyclopaedias can be wrong or out of date.» What should you do? | Throw them away | Believe every word | Only read the pictures | *Check when they were written
exercise: «She is a walking encyclopaedia.» What does this phrase mean? | *A person who knows a huge amount | A map of the city streets | A very slow walker
test: Why should you check online encyclopaedias in more than one source? | Because they are always written in Latin | *Because anyone can edit them | Because they have no words | Because they cost too much
test: Finn says a website must be true because it is online. What is the best answer? | Trust it because it is on a tablet | Ignore all facts | Only believe the pictures | *Check the claim in another source too
exam: Which order are encyclopaedia entries usually arranged in? | Random order | Colour order | Length order | *Alphabetical order
